  "text": "We are given the decimal number **164** and need to find the **base** in which it can be represented as a **three-digit number** with the **middle digit twice the last digit**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure of the number\n\nLet the number be represented in base $ b $ as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Digit1 (hundreds place)} \\times b^2 + \\text{Digit2 (tens place)} \\times b + \\text{Digit3 (units place)} = 164\n$$\n\nSince it's a **three-digit number**, the hundreds digit (Digit1) must be **non-zero**.\n\nWe are told:\n\n- Digit2 (middle digit) = 2 × Digit3 (units digit)\n- The number is 164 in decimal.\n\nLet:\n\n- $ a = \\text{Digit1} $\n- $ b = \\text{Digit2} $\n- $ c = \\text{Digit3} $\n\nWe know:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b^2 + b \\cdot c + c = 164\n$$\n\nAlso:\n\n$$\nb = 2c \\Rightarrow b = 2c\n$$\n\nSo we can substitute $ b = 2c $ into the equation:\n\n$$\na \\cdot (2c)^2 + 2c \\cdot c + c = 164\n$$\n\n$$\na \\cdot 4c^2 + 2c^2 + c = 164\n$$\n\nLet’s factor $ c $:\n\n$$\nc(4ac^2 + 2c + 1) = 164\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Try possible values of $ c $\n\nSince $ c $ is the **units digit**, it must be a **digit in base $ b $**, so:\n\n- $ c \\in \\{1, 2, 3, ..., b-1\\} $\n\nTry $ c = 1 $:\n\nThen $ b = 2c = 2 $, so the number is:\n\n$$\na \\cdot 2^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 1 = 4a + 3 = 164 \\Rightarrow 4a = 161 \\Rightarrow a = \\frac{161}{4}\n$$\n\nNot an integer. So **not valid**.\n\nTry $ c = 2 $:\n\nThen $ b = 4 $, and the number is:\n\n$$\na \\cdot 4^2 + 4 \\cdot 2 + 2 = 16a + 10 = 164 \\Rightarrow 16a = 154 \\Rightarrow a = \\frac{154}{16} = 9.625\n$$\n\nNot an integer. So **not valid**.\n\nTry $ c = 3 $:\n\nThen $ b = 6 $, number is:\n\n$$\na \\cdot 6^2 + 6 \\cdot 3 + 3 = 36a + 18 + 3 = 36a + 21 = 164 \\Rightarrow 36a = 143 \\Rightarrow a = \\frac{143}{36} \\approx 3.97\n$$\n\nNot an integer. So **not valid**.\n\nTry $ c = 4 $:\n\nThen $ b = 8 $, number is:\n\n$$\na \\cdot 8^2 + 8 \\cdot 4 + 4 = 64a + 32 + 4 = 64a + 36 = 164 \\Rightarrow 64a = 128 \\Rightarrow a = 2\n$$\n\nNow check if this works:\n\n- $ a = 2 $\n- $ b = 8 $\n- $ c = 4 $\n\nSo the number in base 8 is:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 8^2 + 8 \\times 4 + 4 = 128 + 32 + 4 = 164\n$$\n\n✅ **Yes!**\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{8}\n$$",
